%I A402463 #10 Nov 26 2026 19:43:08
%S A402463 1565,730337,10255589,211311053,1036623433,2917776089,4293786577,
%T A402463 7743451669,14214080273,24136583417,48077551237,157938913261,
%U A402463 136633022353,224967577997,356472105833,414055448641,878354533697,864186650077,879565293881,2563873319593
%N A402463 Square roots of the central entries in a six-square 3 X 3 magic-square construction from Pythagorean primes using fourth powers of the hypotenuse and the difference of the legs, for the primes where all nine entries are positive.
%C A402463 For each p = A002144(k), let q = A402462(k) and Q = p*q.
%C A402463 There are unique integers x > y > 0 such that p = x^2 + y^2. Let u = x^2 - y^2, v = 2*x*y and h = |u-v|. Here u is the odd leg and v is the even leg of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p.
%C A402463 Let C = Q^2, X = p^3*h^2, Y = p*(h^4-p^4)/2, d = -2*u*v*q^2, e = 2*X*Y and R = h*(h^4-3*p^4)/2.
%C A402463 The sequence lists Q for the primes p with R^2 < 2*C, in increasing order of p. If k(n) is the n-th index k for which this holds, then a(n) = A002144(k(n))*A402462(k(n)). The value q may be prime or composite.
%C A402463 For these p, the array with rows
%C A402463   [C-e,     C+d, C-d+e],
%C A402463   [C-d+2*e, C,   C+d-2*e],
%C A402463   [C+d-e,   C-d, C+e]
%C A402463 is a 3 X 3 magic square with magic sum 3*C. The six entries C-e, C+d, C, C+d-2*e, C-d and C+e are respectively (X-Y)^2, (q*h)^2, Q^2, R^2, (q*(u+v))^2 and (X+Y)^2.
%C A402463 Eight of the nine entries are positive for every Pythagorean prime p; the ninth, C-d+2*e = 2*C-R^2, is positive exactly when R^2 < 2*C, the condition used here. It holds for about 67.4% of all Pythagorean primes in the limit. All nine entries are distinct; see the linked note for the proof.
%C A402463 The upper left entry is A400365(k(n))^2, since X-Y = p*(u^4+v^4).
%C A402463 The terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of Q; for example, a(12) = 157938913261 > a(13) = 136633022353.
%C A402463 Since gcd(p,q) = 1, Q is a product of two distinct primes exactly when the corresponding p belongs to A402461.
%C A402463 For background on 3 X 3 magic squares with six square entries, see Morgenstern's discussion of Euler's formulas and Boyer's work linked below.
%H A402463 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402463/b402463.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1714</a>
%H A402463 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A402463 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A402463 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A402463 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A402463 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402463/a402463.txt">Half-sums of fourth powers using the difference of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A402463 Let p be the n-th Pythagorean prime, in increasing order, for which h^2*(h^4-3*p^4)^2 < 2*p^2*(p^4+h^4)^2, where h = |u-v| and u, v are the legs of its primitive Pythagorean triangle. Then a(n) = p*(p^4+h^4)/2.
%F A402463 With k(n) as in Comments, a(n) = A002144(k(n))*A402462(k(n)).
%F A402463 a(n) = 4*A002144(k(n))^5 - 2*A400365(k(n)) - A402008(k(n)).
%e A402463 For p = 5, q = (5^4+1^4)/2 = 313 and a(1) = 5*313 = 1565. The construction gives
%e A402463   [   1685^2,  313^2, 4410481 ]
%e A402463   [  4020481, 1565^2,   937^2 ]
%e A402463   [   487969, 2191^2,  1435^2 ]
%e A402463 Every row, column and diagonal sums to 3*1565^2 = 7347675. All nine entries are positive and distinct, and exactly six of them are squares.
%e A402463 For p = 13, q = 15481, Q = 201253 and R = -291487. The middle left entry is 2*201253^2 - 291487^2 = -3959131151, so Q = 201253 is omitted.
%e A402463 The next Pythagorean prime, 17, gives q = 42961, Q = 730337 and R = -868567. Since 2*730337^2 - 868567^2 = 312375633649 > 0, a(2) = 730337. Here k(2) = 3.
%o A402463 (Python)
%o A402463 from math import isqrt
%o A402463 from sympy import primerange
%o A402463 def a402463(limit=320):
%o A402463     # Return terms for qualifying hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A402463     out = []
%o A402463     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A402463         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A402463             continue
%o A402463         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A402463             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A402463             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A402463                 h = abs(x*x - y*y - 2*x*y)
%o A402463                 q = (p**4 + h**4) // 2
%o A402463                 Q = p*q
%o A402463                 R = h*(h**4 - 3*p**4) // 2
%o A402463                 if R*R < 2*Q*Q:
%o A402463                     out.append(Q)
%o A402463                 break
%o A402463     return out
%Y A402463 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445, A401446, A401740, A401741, A401742, A402006, A402007, A402008, A402461, A402462.
